*Who is at risk.* I started this project expecting to confirm that detectors are unfair to writers like me. In these data they are not, at least once topic and setting are held fixed. English learners' essays carry spelling mistakes, unusual word choices and uneven sentences, and these make a text less predictable to a language model and less like the machine-written examples a classifier learned from. The human texts that detectors mistake for AI are of three other kinds: short texts; formulaic ones, such as timed-test answers built from stock phrases and heavily edited showcase essays; and texts that a language model has touched, even lightly. Exactly which polished texts a detector flags differs from one detector to another, as the opposite quality trends of Fakespot and Desklib show. So the bias that #cite(<liang2023>, form: "prose") found is real for the kind of text they tested, but it is better described as a bias against short, formulaic or machine-smoothed writing than against a group of writers.

*Proofreading with AI.* The grammar experiment points to a risk that nobody warns students about. Many writing tools now offer AI rewriting right next to ordinary spellchecking, and the two look like the same kind of help. They are not, as far as detectors are concerned. A student who accepts an AI tool's grammar fixes on an essay they wrote themselves can turn it from an essay few detectors flag into one that many flag, while a dictionary spellcheck barely changes the verdict. Whether such an essay should count as "AI-written" is a question for schools, but a detector cannot tell it apart from one a model wrote, and the student may not know that the choice of tool matters.

*Why this matters for admissions.* Application essays are probably the most edited texts a student ever writes. They go through many drafts, teachers, counselors and grammar checkers. In this study, published examples of successful essays, the kind applicants read and imitate, were flagged by several detectors at rates no admissions office would accept. A detector that is nearly perfect at telling ordinary school essays from AI essays can still be wrong about exactly these texts, because they are unlike the human writing it was trained on. For me as an applicant, the practical lesson is simple: proofread with a plain spellchecker, keep my drafts, and do not let an AI tool "fix" an essay I wrote. For admissions readers, a flag on an application essay should not be read as evidence about its author without other information.

*What vendors' claims can and cannot show.* Reports that a detector has no false positives on Liang et al.'s 91 TOEFL essays @emi2024 @gptzero2023 test a single small set that was published three years ago. The results here suggest checking detectors on human text that varies in quality and amount of editing, with groups matched on topic, rather than on one benchmark of non-native writing.

*Recommendations.* For schools and universities: do not act on a detector flag alone; calibrate any detector on human writing from the same setting and report its false-positive rate there; treat short, formulaic and AI-proofread writing as known sources of false positives; and tell students that AI grammar correction can get an essay they wrote flagged. For detector developers: include edited and high-scoring human writing, and short texts, among the human training and test data.

= Limitations

The study uses only open detectors. Commercial detectors used by schools, such as Turnitin, GPTZero and Pangram, may behave differently; testing them on the same matched essays is the most important next step. The zero-shot detectors use smaller language models than their original papers, and all texts are truncated to 512 tokens. The ELL label in PERSUADE comes from US school records and covers students who are learning English inside the US school system; international applicants writing in English as a foreign language may differ. Liang et al.'s college essays are published examples, not a random sample of applications, and they may appear in the training data of the language models behind some detectors. The PERSUADE and ELLIPSE essays have been public since 2022 and may also have been seen in training, which would lower their apparent AI-likeness; this affects ELL and native essays from the same corpus alike. The AI essays come from 2023 models. Grammar correction was done by a local open model, not by a commercial grammar checker, and is a proxy for that use.

